format session-log-v1
stamp 20260822.014628
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title The prose, the model, and the mechanism sentence
prompt Keaton stopped the loop to ask four things: what the Caravan lift arc actually does, whether Opus 5's reported prose regression is hurting our records enough to switch to Sonnet 4.6 or Opus 4.6 or to call a standfast, whether we should still print batons every round, and whether session logs waste monthly usage.
think Answer the lift question from the tree rather than from the prose. Read six lift commits and one rung file. The mechanism is one shared file, caravan/ladder_checks.rye, holding each lifted body once, with a stub in every rung that passes @This() as a comptime type parameter so the shared body reaches back into that rung's own constants, error set, and types. Scope is within Caravan, not tree-wide.
think Name the principle honestly. It is one-fact-one-place; sameness is only the detector, and the arc has already proven the detector lossy twice -- the queue ranks by gross carried lines rather than net return, and it keys families on whole body text, so one keyword and one comment word split a family of forty-seven into three.
think Read the cited sources rather than reason from memory of them. Issue 77136 is squarely about prose -- forced metaphor, invented aphorism, the word load-bearing, negation-led sentences. Issue 84672 is about non-converging fix spirals, and its own author found the confound: claude-opus-5[1m] plus max effort plus a 110KB CLAUDE.md plus deferred compaction. HN returned 429 and was not read.
think Check whether this pier sits in the 84672 regime. Model is claude-opus-5 with no [1m] variant, CLAUDE_EFFORT is medium, no autoCompactWindow override. We are not in that regime, so 84672 does not apply here.
think Check whether 77136 applies. It does. Our own commit bodies for the note_path fold contain no occurrence of file, function, parameter, or import; the mechanism is genuinely absent from the prose. That is the measurement that matters more than the thread -- Keaton read those commits and could not reconstruct the change.
think Diagnose cause before prescribing cure. The style stack aims at metaphor: Radiant rewards the earned line, the commit rule asks for Radiant bodies, titles are drawn as poetry. A model that follows instructions well follows those hard. Opus 5 amplifies; the rules aim. So the fix is a rule, not a model swap.
think Weigh the model decision by the tree's own compass. Reds-first books nothing -- the choir is GREEN at 107, every fold's arithmetic prediction matched measurement exactly, and the compiler caught a widening the survey missed. Lindy-first says readable commit bodies across three thousand commits outlast any model choice. The risk is asymmetric: a prose rule is reversible on any lap, a mid-arc model swap surfaces as reds across 110 files.
obs Propose the mechanism sentence as one small law: every commit body and every session log names the change in ordinary engineering words -- file, function, parameter, type, import, call -- in at least one plain sentence before any metaphor. It is greppable, so a witness can hold it, and it extends Radiant's own name-the-plain-function-on-first-use from terms to changes.
obs Batons are already retired in practice and nobody noticed. Zero of the last twenty logs mention one; counsel closed 20260821.174047 and the BATON block belonged to that workflow. What carries lap to lap is the recommend line plus the README row, exactly as the loop seed says. Keep the recommend line, drop any block that restates it, keep batons for context-reset handoffs.
obs Measured the token question rather than guessing it. The last forty logs total 216,386 bytes, about 5,410 each, roughly 1,350 output tokens per log -- on the order of one to two percent of a fold lap that edits forty-seven files and sings a 107-witness choir. The rules load is the larger number by far: 117,056 bytes across 37 files in .claude/rules, carried on every request rather than every round.
loom logs=40 bytes=216386 mean_bytes=5410 est_tokens_each=1350
loom rules_files=37 rules_bytes=117056 claude_md_bytes=4627
file session-logs/20260822-014628_the-prose-the-model-and-the-mechanism-sentence.kyri this ruling round's own record; no code changed
recommend check-in the mechanism-sentence rule is a new law and wants Keaton's word before seating. Seat it as one small round, leave the model on claude-opus-5 unchanged, and restart the loop. A rules-consolidation survey over the 37 files in .claude/rules is the second door, and it is worth more tokens than any log ever cost. If a measurement is wanted instead of an argument, run the next two folds on two models at the same rota row and compare the commits.
